---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import HelpFooter from '../components/HelpFooter.astro';
import TrackNav from '../components/TrackNav.astro';
import { DAY2_DONE } from '../data/day2-done';
import { inlineMd } from '../lib/inline-md';

// Day 2 of the two-day training, written for the participant. The block order,
// times and "done when" lines follow the instructor curriculum (/schulung, Day 2);
// this page links to the detail pages rather than repeating them. The end-of-day
// checklist is shared data (src/data/day2-done.ts), not a second copy.

const RULE_LINE =
  '- `npm run check:stories` — every story, rendered in Chromium, axe-checked';
const GENERATION_PROMPT = `Generate the <FRAMEWORK> implementation of <ComponentName> with its test and
its stories, from my handoff document <path or pasted text>, not from the
Figma picture alone. Do not write any other file.

The handoff document's "Deliberate differences" are given constraints, not
mistakes to correct: where the code differs from the design on purpose, build
it that way.

Every prop name matches the handoff document's sections one-for-one: add no
prop and rename no prop. Follow the atelier-component skill for tokens,
composition, accessibility and stories. If a requirement has no natural
mapping onto what you are generating, ask instead of inventing a workaround.
When you are done, run the Definition of Done checks from CLAUDE.md (the ones /verify runs) and
report each exit code.`;
const DOCS_SHOW_CLI = `npx storybook tools --config-dir workshop-<fw>/.storybook --json docs show --input '{"id":"<id>"}'`;
const LINT_PROMPT = `Run figma_lint_design on my component set <node id> with only the rules
hardcoded-color, default-name and detached-component. Return the findings as a
list. Do not change anything in the file.`;
const PARITY_PROMPT = `Run figma_check_design_parity on the node from my handoff document against
my component. Sort every discrepancy into one of four: code (the code should
change), Figma (the master should change), kept on purpose (only if the
handoff document's "Deliberate differences" section names it) or tool artefact
(the tool compared two things that are not the same thing, so there is no
difference to fix; say what it compared). Give the reason in one line each.
Then list what the tool did not compare at all. Do not fix anything yet.`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Build your own component (Day 2)"
  description="Day 2 of the Atelier training, for participants: choose a component brief, design it in Figma, write the handoff, generate component and stories, iterate, verify and finish with a checklist."
  currentPath="/build-your-own"
  tocItems={[
    { id: 'goal', label: 'What you will have' },
    { id: 'block-00', label: '00 · Choose your brief' },
    { id: 'block-01', label: '01 · Design and handoff' },
    { id: 'block-02', label: '02 · Component and stories' },
    { id: 'block-03', label: '03 · Iterate' },
    { id: 'block-04', label: '04 · Verify' },
    { id: 'block-05', label: '05 · Show and transfer' },
    { id: 'done', label: 'Are you done?' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">
    <PageHero
      time="Day 2 · 09:00–17:30"
      title="Build your own component"
      titleAccent="own"
      ledeMax="620px"
    >
      Day 1 you ran the loop on a component we gave you. Today you run it on one
      you design yourself: a brief, a Figma component set, a handoff document,
      then component and stories, iterated until every check is green. You
      prompt; you do not type the code.
    </PageHero>

    <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="goal">
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="goal">What you will have</h2>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          An own component in your duplicate of the Atelier file: a component
          set with variants, properties and dark mode
        </li>
        <li>Component and stories in your own workspace, in your framework</li>
        <li>An a11y audit passed, and dark mode working</li>
      </ul>
      <Callout tone="tip" title="Keep these open all day">
        <p>
          The <strong>Course kit</strong> in the sidebar links the pages you
          reach for most:
          <a href="/briefs" class="docs-link-primary">component briefs</a>, the
          <a href="/prompts#rubric" class="docs-link-primary">prompt rubric</a>,
          the
          <a href="/workshop#glossary" class="docs-link-primary">glossary</a>
          and the
          <a href="/troubleshooting#still-red" class="docs-link-primary"
            >rescue path</a
          >. Nobody continues red: if a step will not go green, use the rescue
          path, pair with a neighbour and tell the trainer right away.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-00">
      <p class="byo-meta">09:00–09:30 · 30 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-00">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">00</span> Choose your brief
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Pick one component: Toast, TagChip, StatCard or Avatar. Each
        <a href="/briefs" class="docs-link-primary">brief</a> says what a
        finished component owes: anatomy, axes, states and accessibility
        obligations. It does not say how to draw it; that is the exercise. Read
        the <a href="/briefs" class="docs-link-primary">shared rules</a> first,
        then your brief:
        <a href="/briefs/toast" class="docs-link-primary">Toast</a>,
        <a href="/briefs/tagchip" class="docs-link-primary">TagChip</a>,
        <a href="/briefs/statcard" class="docs-link-primary">StatCard</a>,
        <a href="/briefs/avatar" class="docs-link-primary">Avatar</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Skills are guides that Claude loads when the task matches; see
        <a href="/agent-skills" class="docs-link-primary">Agent skills</a>. Now
        look at what the agent reads in your workspace. Three files (3 min on
        the projector):
      </p>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, its "Definition of Done": the rule the agent
          reads every session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>.claude/skills/atelier-component/SKILL.md</code>, the
          <code>description</code> in its frontmatter: when the skill loads.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>.claude/settings.json</code>, <code>hooks.PostToolUse</code>,
          which runs <code>.claude/hooks/format-edited.sh</code>: it runs after
          every edit without being asked.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        The trainer shows once what the agent does with the rules file: ask
        "Which commands does this workspace's Definition of Done list? Only list
        them." (six), delete the line below from the list, run
        <code>/clear</code> and ask again (five, and
        <code>npm run check:stories</code> is gone), then put the line back. You
        do not edit <code>CLAUDE.md</code> here.
      </p>
      <Code code={RULE_LINE} lang="markdown" title="CLAUDE.md" />
      <p class="docs-step-desc">Your exercise (6 min):</p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Ask the same question: "Which commands does this workspace's
          Definition of Done list? Only list them."
        </li>
        <li>
          Write down the one line your own team would add, in the same shape as
          the line above: the command, a dash, what it proves.
        </li>
        <li>
          Compare with your neighbour: is it a command that can fail, or a wish?
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        The optional
        <a href="/first-component#bridge" class="docs-link-primary"
          >bridge exercise</a
        >
        (add a variant to your SettingsCard, with story) asks for the same work
        as Block 02. If you have not done it, skim it: it is cheaper to meet
        that work on a component that already works.
      </p>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> you know your target component, can name the
        workspace's skills, and have written the one line your own team would
        add to the rules file: a command and what it proves, checked with a
        neighbour as a command that can fail, not a wish.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-01">
      <p class="byo-meta">09:30–11:00 · 90 min · high-risk block</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-01">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span> Design in Figma and
        write the handoff
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        The handoff document is the prompt's input, so the quality of this block
        decides the rest of the day.
      </p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Open the prepared duplicate of the Atelier file on your machine, the
          one copy used on both days, and never change the original file. Do not
          duplicate again: a second copy has a new file key, and
          <code>package.json</code> carries the key.
        </li>
        <li>
          Start from your brief's starter frame on the Workshop Templates page
          (it is part of the prepared duplicate): Toast / Starter, StatCard /
          Starter, TagChip / Starter or Avatar / Starter. Fills, and where the
          component has them paddings and radii, are already bound to Library
          Tokens variables, so the first step is copy and rename, not an empty
          canvas.
        </li>
        <li>
          Plan 5 min for opening the duplicate, 20 min to draw the set from the
          starter frame (layout, variants, token assignment: the starter frame
          adapted, not a design from scratch), 20 min to structure it through
          MCP (component properties and a11y audits), 5 min for the Figma
          linter, 15 min for the API rules with a short exercise, 22 min for the
          handoff document and a 3 min recap at the end.
        </li>
        <li>
          Aim for at least 2 variants × 2 states, properties, correct
          <code>--ui-*</code> tokens and a dark-mode variant. The exact matrix
          is in your brief.
        </li>
        <li>
          API rules first (15 min: about 5 on the rules, about 10 on a short
          exercise on your own component), because the handoff fixes the prop
          names and values: predictable naming, literal unions, composition over
          config, no hidden defaults. The result of the exercise is the property
          list your handoff document takes over.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run the Figma linter on your own component set (5 min). Ask the agent
          to run <code>figma_lint_design</code> with only three rules:
          <code>hardcoded-color</code>, <code>default-name</code> and
          <code>detached-component</code>.
          <Code
            code={LINT_PROMPT}
            lang="text"
            wrap
            title="Figma linter prompt"
          />
          Sort each finding: real or false alarm. Fix the real ones before the
          handoff document and note the false alarms. The linter does not tell
          you whether the design is right.
        </li>
        <li>
          Before anyone prompts, write a short handoff document with nine items:
          file URL and node id, chosen variants and states, token bindings,
          reuse versus rebuild, the behaviour from the brief, explicit
          exclusions, target files, acceptance criteria and deliberate
          differences. The last one says what the code will do differently from
          the design on purpose, and why. For example, a
          <code>State=Hover</code> variant in Figma is <code>:hover</code> in
          CSS, not a property, and a Figma Boolean "Show icon" is a slot in
          code. It is a checklist, not a template.
          <a href="/design-to-code#handoff-example" class="docs-link-primary"
            >A filled handoff document</a
          >
          shows one.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <Callout tone="warning" title="If something fails">
        <p>
          Bridge not connected, node id does not resolve, port conflicts: see
          <a href="/troubleshooting" class="docs-link-primary"
            >Troubleshooting</a
          >. Still red: the
          <a href="/troubleshooting#still-red" class="docs-link-primary"
            >rescue path</a
          >.
        </p>
      </Callout>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> you have a master plus a filled handoff
        document, including its deliberate differences.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-02">
      <p class="byo-meta">11:15–12:30 · 75 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-02">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span> Component and stories
        from the handoff
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        One prompt turns the handoff document into a component you can look at.
        Prompt from the handoff document, not from the picture alone: behaviour
        and exclusions come from the document.
      </p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Start <code>npm run storybook</code> if it is not running, and run
          <code>/mcp</code>: <code>storybook-local</code> shows as connected.
          Check that the dev and test tools
          (<code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code>,
          <code>stories-preview</code>, <code>test-run</code>) are listed. Not
          connected means Storybook is not running: start it, then
          <code>/mcp</code> and reconnect if the tools still do not appear.
          <code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code> is mandatory before
          every <code>*.stories.*</code> edit.
        </li>
        <li>
          Switch to plan mode (Shift+Tab until the status line shows it), send
          the generation prompt with your handoff document as the input, and
          read the plan before anything is built: every prop in it must be a
          prop of the handoff document. Then let it build. One step, not three:
          Claude writes the component (with its test) and the stories. One story
          per variant value and Boolean state, and a <code>play</code> for every
          behaviour line of the document. The deliberate differences go into the
          prompt as they are; the component declares its prop types directly.
          <Code
            code={GENERATION_PROMPT}
            lang="text"
            wrap
            title="generation prompt"
          />
        </li>
        <li>
          Look at the result. Open every story in the running Storybook (or
          through <code>stories-preview</code>) and hand what is wrong back to
          Claude by prompt. Do not type the code. Background on stories and the
          MCP docs: <a href="/storybook" class="docs-link-primary">Storybook</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          What can the agent read about your component (10 min)? Call
          <code>docs-list</code>, then <code>docs-show</code> on your own
          component through <code>storybook-local</code>. Read it as an agent
          that has never seen the component and has only this text: what would
          it have to guess or get wrong? The descriptions are usually there
          already, because the agent wrote them while it generated, so look for
          what is left: a code snippet that would not work as shown, a value
          whose effect is not stated, behaviour that is documented nowhere. Sort
          the list (a gap in the documentation, or a gap in the component), ask
          Claude to close the documentation gaps where they live (JSDoc, the
          story), then call <code>docs-show</code> again and compare. The second
          call normally shows the new text within about a second; restart
          Storybook only if it does not. Without the MCP, the same call through
          the CLI:
          <Code
            code={DOCS_SHOW_CLI}
            lang="shell"
            wrap
            title="docs-show without the MCP"
          />
          What makes these descriptions complete is on
          <a href="/storybook-readiness" class="docs-link-primary"
            >Storybook readiness</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Look at both (8 min). The trainer first shows, on one story, the
          Design panel: the Figma frame as an embed (it may ask for a Figma
          login) and a PNG of the frame (it shows without one). The story takes
          its Figma link from the Source line of your handoff document. Then put
          the running stories next to the Figma component and go through every
          variant and state, one pass. Write the differences down as a list;
          Block 04 sorts it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Commit when the block is green:
          <code
            >git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m "day 2 block 02: green"</code
          >. Block 03 falls back to this commit if it goes wrong.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> the component from your handoff document runs
        in Storybook with one story per variant value, and you have read
        <code>docs-show</code> on it as an agent that has only that text and
        closed the gaps it would have had to guess.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-03">
      <p class="byo-meta">13:30–14:30 · 60 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-03">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span> Iterate: prompt
        quality and the self-healing loop
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        A single prompt is not the result. Write prompts, judge the output,
        correct it, and do not type the code yourself.
      </p>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          Judge every prompt against the six-check
          <a href="/prompts#rubric" class="docs-link-primary">prompt rubric</a>
          before you send it. When a revision is needed, the failed check is the
          reason you state.
        </li>
        <li>
          Revise the prompt or the context: a one-off goes into the next prompt;
          a mistake that would recur goes into CLAUDE.md or the skill, with its
          reason. The skill and stylelint enforce the <code>--ui-*</code>
          tokens.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run the test loop: <code>stories-preview</code> and
          <code>test-run</code> (with a11y) through <code>storybook-local</code
          >, or <code>npm run check:stories</code>, which runs every story in
          Chromium with play assertions and axe. <code>npm test</code>
          (<code>check:unit</code>) covers only the unit half.
        </li>
        <li>
          Paste error messages back to Claude instead of debugging yourself. The
          loop is described in
          <a href="/design-to-code#self-healing-loop" class="docs-link-primary"
            >the self-healing loop</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Undo rule: commit when the stories are green
          (<code>git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m "&lt;step&gt;: green"</code
          >). After two failed fixes, go back to the last green commit with
          <code
            >git reset --hard HEAD &amp;&amp; git clean -fd --
            workshop-&lt;fw&gt;/src</code
          >
          then re-diagnose before the next prompt (preview the delete with
          <code>git clean -nd -- workshop-&lt;fw&gt;/src</code>).
          <code>reset --hard</code> restores every tracked file to the last
          green commit; <code>git checkout -- .</code> alone would leave the new
          component and story files the agent created, so the gates would stay
          red. The <code>clean</code> is limited to your app's <code>src/</code>
          and never uses <code>-x</code>, so ignored files and anything outside
          it survive.
        </li>
        <li>
          The trainer hands out the golden prompts for this block on site.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> your stories are green and you revised the
        prompt at least once, with the reason stated.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-04">
      <p class="byo-meta">14:45–16:00 · 75 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-04">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span> Verify: a11y, states,
        parity
      </h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">A quiet report is not a clean component.</p>
      <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
        <li>
          Three-step a11y check: <code>figma_audit_component_accessibility</code
          >, then the Storybook a11y panel (axe-core), then a keyboard
          walkthrough. Read how the audit classified the component, not only the
          score: a set classed as presentational skips the focus-indicator and
          target-size checks, so 100 out of 100 can have checked little. The
          keyboard walkthrough is yours, by hand: Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter, Space,
          Escape. Let Claude fix the findings. Background:
          <a href="/a11y-workflow#a11y-in-the-loop" class="docs-link-primary"
            >a11y in the loop</a
          >
          and
          <a href="/accessibility" class="docs-link-primary">Accessibility</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check hover, active and disabled states, and the focus ring via
          tokens.
        </li>
        <li>
          Dark mode: use the theme control in the Storybook toolbar. It sets
          <code>data-theme</code>, exactly the selector <code>tokens.css</code>
          switches on. Backgrounds are canvas colours, not a theme. Note that
          axe runs only in light mode in <code>check:stories</code>: the toggle
          is a preview, not test coverage.
        </li>
        <li>
          Parity report on your own component (8 min). Run
          <code>figma_check_design_parity</code> with the node id from your
          handoff document; it needs the connected Desktop Bridge, and without
          it there is no parity call. A quiet report is not a clean component:
          what the call does not compare, it does not report. Expect a low score
          and a short real list: the tool marks every finding as a code change,
          and several are artefacts of the comparison (a resolved hex against a
          <code>var(--ui-*)</code> string, the bounding box of the whole variant
          set against one element, a property reported missing on both sides
          because Figma's name carries an <code>#id</code> suffix). Read the
          result with
          <a href="/design-to-code#parity-report" class="docs-link-primary"
            >reading a parity report</a
          >.
          <Code code={PARITY_PROMPT} lang="text" wrap title="parity prompt" />
        </li>
        <li>
          Sort every difference (20 min): the list from the parity report plus
          the list from Block 02, one label each: <strong>code</strong> (hand it
          back to Claude; fixed inside this row), <strong>Figma</strong> (a note
          for the designer; the master is not changed now),
          <strong>kept on purpose</strong> (it must be in the handoff document's
          "Deliberate differences"; if it is not there, either write it there
          and say why, or it is code or Figma) or <strong>tool artefact</strong>
          (the tool compared two things that are not the same thing; nothing to
          fix, but say what it compared with what, and check two such lines
          yourself). Classic mislabel: "fixing" in code what was kept on
          purpose. Without the bridge there is no parity call: sort the Block 02
          list.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> the component works by keyboard and has no
        open accessibility finding, dark mode switches through tokens, and every
        difference to Figma is sorted (code / Figma / kept on purpose / tool
        artefact).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section byo-block" aria-labelledby="block-05">
      <p class="byo-meta">16:10–17:30 · 80 min</p>
      <h2 class="docs-section-title" id="block-05">
        <span class="byo-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span> Show and tell, then
        transfer to your own design system
      </h2>
      <ul class="byo-list">
        <li>
          Cohorts have up to 8 participants, so each person gets strictly 3
          minutes. Show your component, one prompt that worked and one that
          failed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Workspace anatomy (12 min), what makes the loop portable, file by
          file: <code>.mcp.json</code> (the servers: in your own repo the
          library half is your library's manifest or package, the local half is
          the addon MCP of the Storybook you already run),
          <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (the working agreement),
          <code>.claude/skills/</code> (<code>atelier-component</code>,
          <code>figma-workspace-architect</code>, the Storybook skills),
          <code>.claude/settings.json</code> (permissions and the format hook),
          <code>.storybook/main.ts</code> (addon-vitest, addon-a11y,
          addon-themes), and the <code>package.json</code> scripts
          (<code>lint</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>check:*</code>). What to
          copy into your own repo, in this order, most portable first:
          <ol class="byo-list byo-list--ordered">
            <li>
              The Storybook MCP on your own Storybook (<code>.mcp.json</code>,
              <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code>): the agent reads your real
              component API.
            </li>
            <li>
              A rules file with the exact check commands (<code>CLAUDE.md</code>
              or <code>AGENTS.md</code>, its "Definition of Done"): the agent
              knows what to run before it says done.
            </li>
            <li>
              Strict TypeScript, ESLint and stylelint: you probably have them;
              let the agent run them and read the output.
            </li>
            <li>
              Story tests with axe (<code>.storybook/main.ts</code>:
              addon-vitest, addon-a11y): every story is a test.
            </li>
            <li>
              One proof command and the hooks
              (<code>.claude/commands/verify.md</code>,
              <code>.claude/hooks/</code>).
            </li>
            <li>Skills (<code>.claude/skills/</code>).</li>
          </ol>
          You take the workspace home as it is on the course machine; the
          trainer tells you how it leaves the machine.
        </li>
        <li>
          What carries your docs, and what you depend on (3 min, talk only): the
          Storybook MCP on your own Storybook is the route that brings your
          design system's docs to the agents of the teams that consume it, and
          the Figma linter, the accessibility audit and the parity check all
          stand on the third-party figma-console-mcp, a dependency you choose.
        </li>
        <li>
          Transfer (27 min): the scorecard "What a design system has to bring"
          is the frame. From memory, mark its seven rows for your own design
          system (yes / partly / no), pick the weakest row and write your next
          step for it, then read a neighbour's step. Homework: run both
          checklists with the check prompt on your own system, the Figma
          checklist and the Storybook checklist. Both are handouts and you take
          both home; the pages
          <a href="/readiness" class="docs-link-primary">readiness</a>,
          <a href="/storybook-readiness#self-check" class="docs-link-primary"
            >Storybook readiness</a
          >
          and
          <a href="/design-principles#check-your-own" class="docs-link-primary"
            >check your own system</a
          >
          hold the how. The pages time-box each full run at 30 minutes; nobody
          starts a checklist in the room.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="byo-done">
        <strong>Done when</strong> you have marked the seven scorecard rows for
        your own design system from memory and leave with a written next step
        for the weakest row, and the follow-up channel is set up.
      </p>
    </section>

